experiment: thrpatch
  instrument        thrpatch   trial seed 0000000001355c08   2000000 ticks per leg
                    another probe is another trial sequence: its numbers
                    are not comparable with these (~0.05 at 300 trials)
  the question      can a snapshot be resumed with a PATCHED per-module
                    genome, and does the patch TAKE EFFECT? It gates
                    `thrfork`, which is 2.6 h; this is minutes.
  the knob          vocal's ip_wake_scale and ip_sleep_scale -> 0. NOT
                    `ip_rate`, which is global and would freeze every
                    module. `scaling_rate` is left alive, so only the
                    threshold path stops.
  what refuses it   load_snapshot returning anything but kOk, OR the
                    patched arm's larynx threshold moving anyway. Loading
                    is not taking effect, and a patch that resumes cleanly
                    and does nothing would otherwise read as a pass.
  Expect::kRead     this cannot fail for a measured reason about the
                    creature -- it is an apparatus check.

  THE PATCH. vocal is module 4; its genome sits at byte 1816 of the
  snapshot's embedded blob (4060 bytes).

  THE CONTROLS, read before the dose.
    state hash reproduced on the unpatched resume   YES
    patched snapshot resumed                        YES
    larynx mean threshold at the fork, both arms    +2.49907 / +2.49907

  THE DOSE. larynx mean threshold moved over the second leg:
    shipped (free)   -0.01992
    patched (frozen) +0.00000

  THE PATCH LOADS AND TAKES EFFECT, so `thrfork` is buildable as designed:
  the frozen arm holds its threshold at 0% of the shipped arm's movement
  while sharing a bit-identical past. Build it.
